TASK.** Three rounds in a row have produced the same class of finding at a new site each time: rev3 P2-C (the running-server rule pinned at the entry only for servers carrying admission data), rev4 P2-B (`Request.Name` free at the entry while the path was pinned at the constants), and now rev5 P2-A (the broker-or-refuse rule pinned at the entry only for the shapes the fixtures happen to build). Each round I handed you the specific tests the reviewer had already measured, you closed exactly those, and the next review found the same class one site over. **That means the defect is your measurement, not your code.** So this round the deliverable is the instrument, and the enumerated items below are only its first outputs.\\n\\nBuild and commit a **gate × entry census**. For every gate in the package, list every production entry that can reach it — `Acquire` foreground, `Acquire` background, the exported `EnsureRuntimeDir`, `VerifyRuntimeDir`, `BuildArgv`, `SocketPath`, `CheckBrokerContact`, `CheckServerAttested` and anything else that composes them. For every (gate, entry) cell, state one of exactly three things: a named committed test that drives the gate THROUGH that entry and an admitting narrowing row that it kills; or `unreachable` with the reason a caller cannot get there; or a stated BOUND naming what is not measured and who owns it. Put the census in the conformance matrix as its own table and mirror its shape in TRACEABILITY. A cell that says \\\"pinned at the helper\\\" and nothing else is exactly the hole the last three reviews walked through — a helper pin plus an entry that reaches the same gate is two cells, not one. Count the cells and report the ratio you MEASURE. If the census is honest and some cells are bounds, that is an acceptable revision; another round of four tests without the census is not.\\n\\nThe four members the reviewer already measured are your first four cells, and the probes named are templates:\\n1. **Broker-side attested-server wiring has no admitting narrowing.** SPEC §4.2 1419-1424 requires the background caller to contact an already-running authenticated broker **and its attested server**, else `capability_unavailable`, never a fallback. Four plants that each admit one realistic member SURVIVED both passes: the zero `RealmAdmission{}` report with a live same-user generation-bound principal (the nothing-reconciled state, the exact shape rev3 closed on the FOREGROUND path); rows present with an empty generation against a bound wanted generation; a decoy-only admission with a bound generation (the member the foreground entry already pins); and a background miss whose `Dependencies` carry the foreground `ProbeServer`, which re-enters foreground creation — realistic, because the lifecycle adapter will pass one shared `Dependencies` value to every caller, and no committed background test arms `ProbeServer`, so the `Spawn`-keyed spy census never sees it. Ship: three subtests on `TestAcquireBackgroundMissReturnsTypedUnavailable` asserting the literal `capability_unavailable`, exit 6, the five typed details and zero spawns; the same three rows mirrored on `TestCheckBrokerContactRefusesEachMissingFact`; a narrowing row at the `CheckBrokerContact` server wiring named under rows 27/28/36; a fail-if-called `ProbeServer` spy armed in every background miss test plus `TestAcquireBackgroundVerifiesWithoutCreating`, shipped as a second labelled additive row `D-background-fallback-foreground`; and the row-31/README sentence extended to say the census arms BOTH foreground dependencies.\\n2. **The absence classifier's reject class is pinned at the entry for mode and containment only.** Widening `runtimeAbsent` to admit `runtime ownership` SURVIVED both passes: no committed background `Acquire` test stages foreign ownership, so a foreign-owned leaf on the background path would return `capability_unavailable` with the custody error redacted as its cause — refusal-to-refusal, but row 52 says an unsafe leaf keeps its custody code as the TOP-LEVEL `*Error`, never the wrapper. Add `TestAcquireBackgroundStagesOwnershipRefusal` (the `effectiveUID` seam is the lever) under row 52, or state the ownership member as helper-pinned only in the census.\\n3. **A censusable \\\"before\\\" that is false.** `acquire.go:140-143` and `results.md:61-62` both claim malformed input, ambient reuse and missing dependencies \\\"refuse before any side effect\\\". A foreground request with `SessionID = \\\"12345\\\"` **creates `<root>/tmux` and calls `ProbeServer` before** refusing `tmux_invalid_arguments at session identity`: the session identity is validated only inside `BuildArgv`, after `EnsureRuntimeDir` and the probe. Either parse the session at the `Acquire` entry before any side effect and add no-directory/no-probe assertions to `TestAcquireForegroundRefusesMalformedSession` (row 45), or reword both sentences to the members that are actually true. Rejection pattern (f), and the third time a sentence with a universal quantifier has cost a round — before you write \\\"every\\\", \\\"all\\\", \\\"never\\\" or \\\"before any\\\", make the census cell that proves it.\\n4. **Three observation sentences for TRACEABILITY**, all cheap: a comma inside the runtime root makes `tmuxEnvSocket` split at the wrong comma so a real-encoding `TMUX` value walks past the collision gate — tmux(1) itself splits at the first comma, so the decoding is faithful and the honest text is \\\"roots containing a comma are outside the TMUX member's encoding\\\" in row 23/B16; a cwd-relative spelling of the derived socket walks past the cleaned-string collision gate, the same bound class as the symlink alias, so B16 should read \\\"symlinked or relative alias\\\"; and `Dependencies.Spawn` is inert on the background path.\\n\\nPROCESS. Keep the candidate UNCOMMITTED; exactly one leaf's worth of delta. Never commit on the Story branch, never edit `.task-board` directly, never self-accept, and do NOT touch `internal/traceability` —